Write the finished section in clear prose. Bring together the findings from the sources relevant to this heading.
Explain the mechanisms, conditions, and limits that matter for the finding. Preserve important figures and context,
and make clear where findings differ.
Lead with the answer to this heading. Write self-contained paragraphs without an extra title, headings, or subsections.
Start with a finding that a retrieved passage supports, and cite that passage in the opening paragraph.
Use the brief outline to leave other sections' findings, examples, and conclusions to those sections.
Write about the subject itself. Discuss gaps in the evidence or what a review did not establish only when the heading or the author's instructions explicitly ask for that discussion. In that case cite the source describing what was measured, omitted, or uncertain. Otherwise keep the findings within the scope of their sources and omit commentary on the review.
Cite factual findings in their own sentences. Never infer wider coverage or a trend from a few examples.
